{
  "slug": "number-of-visible-people-in-a-queue",
  "title": "Number of Visible People in a Queue",
  "topic": "stack",
  "difficulty": "Hard",
  "pattern": "monotonic decreasing stack",
  "statement": "A queue of `n` people is standing in one straight line, and `heights[i]` is the height of the person at position `i`. Person `i` can see person `j` (with `j > i`) when no person standing strictly between them is taller than the shorter of the two: the maximum height among the positions `i + 1` up to `j - 1` must be at most `min(heights[i], heights[j])`. Because of that rule a person always sees the person standing immediately in front of them, and someone exactly as tall as the next person does not block the view. Return an array `answer` of length `n` where `answer[i]` is the number of people person `i` can see among the positions after them.\n\nFor example with `heights = [10, 6, 8, 5, 11, 9]` the answer is `[3, 1, 2, 1, 1, 0]`.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= heights.length <= 200` and `1 <= heights[i] <= 10000`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "heights = [1]",
      "output": "[0]",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    },
    {
      "input": "heights = [4890, 8318, 9978, 3259]",
      "output": "[1, 1, 1, 0]",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(heights: number[])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "For a fixed person, only one number matters while you move forward: the tallest height you have passed since that person. A candidate is visible exactly when that running maximum is not larger than both heights.",
    "Once you meet somebody at least as tall as person i, nobody beyond them can be visible to i, so the scan can stop there even though that person is still counted.",
    "For the linear pass keep a stack of indices whose heights strictly decrease. A new person pops everyone no taller than himself, and each popped index gains one visible person; the index left on top then gains one more, and the new person is pushed."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force fixes a person `i` and walks down the line while keeping `mx`, the tallest height seen so far strictly between `i` and the current person `j`. Person `j` is visible exactly when `mx <= min(heights[i], heights[j])`, and the walk stops at the first person whose height is at least `heights[i]`, since from that point on the in-between maximum is already as large as `heights[i]` and nobody further down can ever be visible again. That is O(n\u00b2) because the inner walk can run the whole line for every person. The optimal pass keeps a stack of indices whose heights strictly decrease from the bottom to the top. When person `i` arrives, every index on top of the stack with height at most `heights[i]` is popped and gains one visible person: `i` is the first person in front of them who is not shorter, and the stack invariant guarantees that everybody between that index and `i` is shorter than it. If the stack is still non-empty after those pops, its new top gains one visible person as well, because all the indices just popped are shorter than both it and `i`. Then `i` is pushed. Each index is pushed and popped at most once, so the whole scan is linear.",
    "why_optimal": "The answer has one number per person, so every solution must touch all n values of the input at least once; a single left-to-right pass with amortised O(1) stack work per person meets that bound. The n <= 200 bound in the constraints is why the O(n\u00b2) walk is still acceptable as a reference solution, while the stack version stays linear at any size.",
    "pitfalls": "Forgetting the extra `+1` for the person left on top of the stack after the popping loop; that increment is the person seen across the stack top. Using `<` instead of `<=` in the pop condition, which leaves equally tall people on the stack and undercounts the people they should be seeing (a person exactly as tall as the next one does not block the view). Ending the brute walk on `heights[j] > heights[i]` instead of `>=`, which keeps counting people hidden behind an equally tall person. Returning the wrong length for `n = 1`, where the answer is the single element `[0]`, and mishandling an empty input list."
  },
